WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.617
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.917 --> 00:00:09.698
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.998 --> 00:00:17.701
Unit twenty-nine: Review: Leading with Purpose. Today you will emphasise key ideas with cleft sentences (It is … that).

4
00:00:18.001 --> 00:00:20.607
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:24.451
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.317 --> 00:00:26.155
Purpose.

7
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:31.135
Purpose means the fundamental reason why someone or something exists.

8
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.959
Purpose gives meaning to difficult work.

9
00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.353
Your turn. Say: Purpose.

10
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.155
Purpose.

11
00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:42.732
Vision.

12
00:00:43.717 --> 00:00:46.854
Vision means a compelling picture of a desired future.

13
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:50.355
Her vision united a divided organisation.

14
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.231
Your turn. Say: Vision.

15
00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:56.940
Vision.

16
00:00:57.683 --> 00:00:58.626
Values.

17
00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:02.545
Values means the principles that guide decisions.

18
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.997
Values are tested when they become inconvenient.

19
00:01:06.583 --> 00:01:09.073
Your turn. Say: Values.

20
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:13.016
Values.

21
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:14.612
Impact.

22
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:19.358
Impact means a significant effect on people or situations.

23
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.878
The programme's impact reached thousands of families.

24
00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:26.147
Your turn. Say: Impact.

25
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:29.897
Impact.

26
00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:31.895
Service.

27
00:01:32.883 --> 00:01:35.324
Service means work done to help others.

28
00:01:35.917 --> 00:01:38.613
Service to the community defined his career.

29
00:01:39.217 --> 00:01:42.055
Your turn. Say: Service.

30
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:45.913
Service.

31
00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:47.651
Legacy.

32
00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:52.124
Legacy means something that continues after one has gone.

33
00:01:52.717 --> 00:01:55.947
A purposeful legacy outlives titles and wealth.

34
00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.191
Your turn. Say: Legacy.

35
00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:03.174
Legacy.

36
00:02:03.917 --> 00:02:05.138
Authenticity.

37
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.438
Authenticity means being genuine and true to one's values.

38
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.862
Authenticity builds deeper trust than charisma.

39
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.079
Your turn. Say: Authenticity.

40
00:02:18.933 --> 00:02:20.318
Authenticity.

41
00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:22.073
Reflection.

42
00:02:23.083 --> 00:02:26.360
Reflection means serious thought about one's experiences.

43
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.820
Reflection helps leaders reconnect with their purpose.

44
00:02:30.417 --> 00:02:33.081
Your turn. Say: Reflection.

45
00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:37.004
Reflection.

46
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.602
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:47.178
Visal, after twenty-nine units on leadership, what single idea matters most?

48
00:02:48.283 --> 00:02:58.691
For me, it is purpose that sustains great leaders. Skills and strategies matter, but purpose explains why we keep going when things become difficult.

49
00:02:59.783 --> 00:03:01.516
Isn't vision the same thing?

50
00:03:02.617 --> 00:03:10.354
Not quite. Vision describes where we are going; it is purpose that explains why the journey is worth making.

51
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.728
And how do leaders stay connected to it?

52
00:03:14.817 --> 00:03:22.984
Through reflection. It is in quiet moments, not in busy meetings, that most leaders rediscover what truly matters to them.

53
00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:25.668
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:26.517 --> 00:03:29.956
Grammar focus: Cleft sentences with It is that.

55
00:03:30.256 --> 00:03:31.431
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:32.117 --> 00:03:34.708
It is purpose that sustains great leaders.

57
00:03:35.617 --> 00:03:39.265
It is in quiet moments that leaders rediscover what matters.

58
00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:42.579
It was her mentor who taught her to listen.

59
00:03:43.533 --> 00:03:54.765
An it-cleft splits a sentence to focus on one element: 'Purpose sustains leaders' → 'It is purpose that sustains leaders.' Use 'who' or 'that' for people, 'that' for things and phrases — never 'what'.

60
00:03:55.517 --> 00:03:59.493
Be careful! Don't say: It is purpose what sustains leaders.

61
00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:04.385
Say: It is purpose that sustains leaders.

62
00:04:04.685 --> 00:04:06.348
It is … that (not 'what').

63
00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:09.739
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:10.533 --> 00:04:11.836
Leading With Why.

65
00:04:12.136 --> 00:04:18.362
Throughout this course, we have examined rhetoric, research, diplomacy, ethics and leadership from many perspectives.

66
00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:25.546
What connects these themes is the recognition that it is not technique alone that makes communication or leadership powerful.

67
00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:34.628
Simon Sinek popularised the idea that inspiring leaders begin with 'why', articulating a purpose before explaining what they do or how they do it.

68
00:04:35.633 --> 00:04:45.307
It is this sense of purpose that enables leaders to remain authentic under pressure, to make difficult ethical choices and to inspire others to serve a cause larger than themselves.

69
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:51.202
Ultimately, it is through purpose, reflection and service that knowledge becomes wisdom.

70
00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:56.070
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:56.917 --> 00:04:59.079
It was my teacher blank encouraged me.

72
00:04:59.379 --> 00:05:02.347
A, what. B, who. C, which.

73
00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.621
The answer is B: who.

74
00:05:08.783 --> 00:05:11.781
Being genuine and true to one's values is blank.

75
00:05:12.081 --> 00:05:15.839
A, authenticity. B, impact. C, service.

76
00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.945
The answer is A: authenticity.

77
00:05:23.083 --> 00:05:25.791
Serious thought about one's experiences is blank.

78
00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:29.559
A, vision. B, legacy. C, reflection.

79
00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.529
The answer is C: reflection.

80
00:05:36.683 --> 00:05:38.938
Choose the correct cleft sentence.

81
00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:46.584
A, It is trust what builds teams.. B, It is trust that builds teams.. C, It is trust builds that teams..

82
00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:52.888
The answer is B: It is trust that builds teams..

83
00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:58.070
According to the reading, Simon Sinek said inspiring leaders begin with…

84
00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:01.280
A, how. B, what. C, why.

85
00:06:04.833 --> 00:06:06.740
The answer is C: why.

86
00:06:07.933 --> 00:06:11.491
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:14.582
What purpose would guide your leadership?

88
00:06:31.383 --> 00:06:33.023
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:33.323 --> 00:07:08.361
The purpose that would guide my leadership is to create opportunities for others to grow. It is through education that my own life changed, so I want to help people, especially young Cambodians, discover and develop their potential. This purpose would shape my decisions: I would invest in mentoring, listen carefully and prioritise long-term impact over personal recognition. When challenges arise, it is this sense of purpose that would remind me why the effort matters.

90
00:07:08.661 --> 00:07:10.347
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:16.085
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and cleft sentences with it is that.

92
00:07:16.385 --> 00:07:20.907
Today's value is purpose. Know why you do what you do, and let it guide you.

93
00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:27.588
Congratulations! You have finished Online C two, Book one. See you in Online C two, Book two!

94
00:07:27.888 --> 00:07:30.924
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

